T3={
"square-footage-calculator":dict(t="Square Footage Calculator – Sq Ft, Sq Yd, Acres & Cost",d="Square footage calculator: enter length and width in feet to get square feet, square yards, square meters, acres and total cost at your price per sq ft.",h="Square Footage Calculator",
f=[("L","Length (ft)",12),("W","Width (ft)",10),("N","Number of identical areas",1),("P","Price per sq ft ($, optional)",0)],
js="""var a=L*W*N;return [['Square feet',a.toFixed(2)],['Square yards',(a/9).toFixed(2)],['Square meters',(a*0.09290304).toFixed(2)],['Acres',(a/43560).toFixed(4)],['Estimated cost','$'+(a*P).toFixed(2)]];""",
g="""<h2>How to calculate square footage</h2><p>For a rectangle or square, square footage is simply <b>length × width</b>, with both measurements in feet. If you measured in inches, divide each side by 12 first (or multiply the inches together and divide by 144).</p><p><b>Example:</b> a 12 × 10 ft bedroom is 12 × 10 = 120 sq ft. That is 120 ÷ 9 = 13.33 square yards (the unit carpet is often priced in) and 120 × 0.0929 = 11.15 square meters.</p><h2>L-shaped and irregular rooms</h2><p>Split the space into rectangles, calculate each one, and add them up. Use the <i>Number of identical areas</i> field when you have several same-size spaces, such as three 10 × 12 ft bedrooms. Other shapes:</p><ul><li>Triangle: ½ × base × height</li><li>Circle: 3.1416 × radius²</li><li>Closets and bump-outs: measure them as their own small rectangles</li></ul><h2>Unit conversions</h2><ul><li>1 square yard = 9 sq ft</li><li>1 square meter = 10.764 sq ft</li><li>1 acre = 43,560 sq ft</li><li>1 sq ft = 144 square inches</li></ul><h2>Estimating cost</h2><p>Enter the installed or material price per square foot to get a quick budget. Remember that flooring, tile and sod need a waste allowance on top of the measured area—use the <a href='/flooring-calculator/'>flooring</a>, <a href='/tile-calculator/'>tile</a> or <a href='/sod-grass-seed-calculator/'>sod</a> calculators for that. For walls, use the <a href='/paint-calculator/'>paint calculator</a>, which subtracts doors and windows.</p><h2>Measuring tips</h2><p>Measure wall to wall at floor level, round up to the nearest inch, and measure twice. In real estate, a home's listed square footage usually counts finished, heated living space only—garages and unfinished basements are typically excluded, and rules vary by state.</p>"""),
"cubic-yard-calculator":dict(t="Cubic Yard Calculator – Cubic Yards, Cubic Feet & Cost",d="Cubic yard calculator: length and width in feet and depth in inches to cubic yards, cubic feet, cubic meters and cost for soil, mulch, gravel or concrete.",h="Cubic Yard Calculator",
f=[("L","Length (ft)",10),("W","Width (ft)",10),("D","Depth (in)",4),("P","Price per cubic yard ($, optional)",0)],
js="""var cf=L*W*D/12;return [['Cubic yards',(cf/27).toFixed(2)],['Cubic feet',cf.toFixed(2)],['Cubic meters',(cf*0.0283168).toFixed(2)],['Estimated cost','$'+(cf/27*P).toFixed(2)]];""",
g="""<h2>Cubic yard formula</h2><p>Bulk materials—topsoil, mulch, gravel, sand and ready-mix concrete—are sold by the cubic yard. Calculate the volume in cubic feet, then divide by 27, because one cubic yard is 3 ft × 3 ft × 3 ft = 27 cubic feet.</p><p><b>Cubic yards = length (ft) × width (ft) × depth (in) ÷ 12 ÷ 27</b></p><p><b>Example:</b> a 10 × 10 ft area filled 4 in deep: 10 × 10 × 4 ÷ 12 = 33.33 cu ft ÷ 27 = 1.23 cubic yards.</p><h2>How much does one cubic yard cover?</h2><ul><li>At 2 in deep: about 162 sq ft</li><li>At 3 in deep: about 108 sq ft</li><li>At 4 in deep: about 81 sq ft</li><li>At 6 in deep: about 54 sq ft</li></ul><h2>Bags vs. bulk</h2><p>A cubic yard equals 13.5 bags of 2 cu ft or 9 bags of 3 cu ft. Once you need more than two or three cubic yards, bulk delivery from a landscape or aggregate yard is usually cheaper than bags—ask about the delivery fee and minimum order.</p><h2>Material-specific calculators</h2><p>Some materials are sold by weight or by the bag, so they need extra data such as density or bag yield. Use the <a href='/concrete-calculator/'>concrete</a>, <a href='/mulch-calculator/'>mulch</a>, <a href='/gravel-calculator/'>gravel</a>, <a href='/topsoil-calculator/'>topsoil</a> or <a href='/sand-calculator/'>sand</a> calculators for bag counts and tons.</p><h2>Order a little extra</h2><p>Loose materials settle and compact, and excavations are rarely perfectly even. Adding 5–10% (more for soil fills deeper than a few inches) avoids a second delivery charge.</p>"""),
"board-foot-calculator":dict(t="Board Foot Calculator – Lumber Board Feet & Cost",d="Board foot calculator: thickness and width in inches, length in feet and quantity to board feet, linear feet and lumber cost.",h="Board Foot Calculator",
f=[("T","Thickness (in)",2),("W","Width (in)",6),("L","Length (ft)",8),("Q","Number of boards",10),("P","Price per board foot ($, optional)",0)],
js="""var b=T*W*L/12;return [['Total board feet',(b*Q).toFixed(2)],['Board feet per board',b.toFixed(2)],['Linear feet',(L*Q).toFixed(1)],['Estimated cost','$'+(b*Q*P).toFixed(2)]];""",
g="""<h2>What is a board foot?</h2><p>A board foot is a volume of wood equal to a board 12 in long, 12 in wide and 1 in thick—144 cubic inches. Hardwood lumberyards and sawmills price rough lumber by the board foot, so you need this number to compare prices and plan a project.</p><p><b>Board feet = thickness (in) × width (in) × length (ft) ÷ 12</b></p><p>If you measure the length in inches, use thickness × width × length ÷ 144 instead.</p><p><b>Example:</b> ten 2 × 6 boards, 8 ft long: 2 × 6 × 8 ÷ 12 = 8 board feet each, 80 board feet in total. At $4.50 per board foot that is $360.</p><h2>Nominal vs. actual size</h2><p>Board footage is normally calculated from <b>nominal</b> dimensions. A softwood “2×6” actually measures about 1½ × 5½ in after drying and planing, but it is still counted as 2 × 6. Hardwood thickness is sold in quarters of an inch: 4/4 (“four quarter”) is 1 in rough, 5/4 is 1¼ in, 8/4 is 2 in. Surfaced boards (S2S or S4S) are usually priced on the rough size before planing—ask your supplier how they measure.</p><h2>Plan for waste</h2><p>Rough hardwood has defects, checks at the ends and wane along the edges. Woodworkers commonly buy 20–30% more board footage than the finished parts list requires; 10–15% extra is typical for clear, surfaced stock.</p><h2>Linear feet vs. board feet</h2><p>Home centers often price dimensional lumber per piece or per linear foot instead. Linear feet only measures length, so a 2×12 and a 2×4 of the same length have the same linear footage but very different board footage.</p>"""),
"concrete-block-calculator":dict(t="Concrete Block Calculator – CMU Blocks & Mortar Bags",d="Concrete block calculator: wall length and height to the number of 8×8×16 cinder blocks (CMU), courses and 80 lb bags of mortar mix.",h="Concrete Block Calculator",
f=[("L","Wall length (ft)",40),("H","Wall height (ft)",4),("B","Block length incl. joint (in)",16),("C","Block height incl. joint (in)",8),("X","Waste (%)",5),("M","Blocks per 80 lb mortar bag",13)],
js="""var a=L*H,per=144/(B*C),n=Math.ceil(a*per*(1+X/100));return [['Blocks incl. waste',n],['Wall area (sq ft)',a.toFixed(1)],['Blocks per sq ft',per.toFixed(3)],['Courses (rows)',Math.ceil(H*12/C)],['80 lb mortar bags',Math.ceil(n/M)]];""",
g="""<h2>How many concrete blocks do I need?</h2><p>A standard concrete masonry unit (CMU, often called a cinder block) is sold by its <b>nominal</b> size, 8 × 8 × 16 in. Its actual size is 7⅝ × 7⅝ × 15⅝ in; the ⅜ in mortar joint makes up the difference. So each block covers 16 × 8 = 128 sq in of wall face, or 1.125 blocks per square foot.</p><p><b>Blocks = wall length (ft) × wall height (ft) × 144 ÷ (block length × block height) + waste</b></p><p><b>Example:</b> a 40 ft long, 4 ft high wall is 160 sq ft × 1.125 = 180 blocks. With 5% waste: 189 blocks in 6 courses. At 13 blocks per 80 lb bag of mortar mix, that is 15 bags.</p><h2>Mortar</h2><p>Quikrete states that one 80 lb bag of its Mortar Mix (Type N) or Mason Mix (Type S) lays up to about 13 standard 8 × 8 × 16 in blocks. Real-world yield is often lower because of joint thickness, mixing water and droppings, so the calculator rounds up and you may want a spare bag. Use Type S for below-grade and retaining applications and Type N for most above-grade walls, unless your plans specify otherwise.</p><h2>Openings, corners and half blocks</h2><ul><li>Subtract door and window openings from the wall area, but add lintels and bond-beam blocks where needed.</li><li>Corners use corner (end) blocks; running bond needs half blocks at the ends of alternate courses.</li><li>Order bottom-course blocks to match your footing length and plan cuts before you start.</li></ul><h2>Footings, reinforcement and code</h2><p>Block walls need a level concrete footing—use the <a href='/concrete-calculator/'>concrete calculator</a> to size it. Load-bearing walls, retaining walls and walls over a few feet tall usually require vertical rebar and grouted cores; follow your local building code or an engineer's design.</p>"""),
"brick-calculator":dict(t="Brick Calculator – How Many Bricks & Mortar Bags?",d="Brick calculator: wall area, brick size and mortar joint to bricks per square foot, total bricks with waste and 80 lb bags of mortar.",h="Brick Calculator",
f=[("A","Wall area (sq ft)",100),("O","Openings to subtract (sq ft)",0),("B","Brick length (in)",7.625),("C","Brick height (in)",2.25),("J","Mortar joint (in)",0.375),("X","Waste (%)",10),("M","Bricks per 80 lb mortar bag",37)],
js="""var a=Math.max(0,A-O),per=144/((B+J)*(C+J)),n=Math.ceil(a*per*(1+X/100));return [['Bricks incl. waste',n],['Net wall area (sq ft)',a.toFixed(1)],['Bricks per sq ft',per.toFixed(2)],['80 lb mortar bags',Math.ceil(n/M)]];""",
g="""<h2>How to estimate bricks</h2><p>Each brick plus its share of mortar covers (brick length + joint) × (brick height + joint) square inches of wall. Divide 144 by that to get bricks per square foot, multiply by the net wall area, and add waste for cuts and breakage.</p><p><b>Example:</b> a US modular brick is 7⅝ × 2¼ in with a ⅜ in joint: (8 × 2.625) = 21 sq in, so 144 ÷ 21 = 6.86 bricks per sq ft. A 100 sq ft wall needs 686 bricks; with 10% waste, 755 bricks.</p><h2>Common US brick sizes (actual, single wythe)</h2><ul><li>Modular: 7⅝ × 2¼ in face — about 6.9 per sq ft with ⅜ in joints</li><li>Queen: 7⅝ × 2¾ in — about 5.8 per sq ft</li><li>King: 9⅝ × 2⅝ in — about 5.0 per sq ft</li><li>Utility: 11⅝ × 3⅝ in — about 3.0 per sq ft</li></ul><p>Sizes vary by manufacturer and region, so enter the dimensions from your supplier's spec sheet. Double-wythe (two layers) walls need twice the bricks.</p><h2>Mortar bags</h2><p>Quikrete rates one 80 lb bag of Mortar Mix or Mason Mix at up to about 37 standard bricks. Larger bricks, thicker joints and inexperienced bricklaying all use more, so buy a bag or two extra. A cubic foot of mortar is roughly the yield of one 80 lb bag (about 0.83 cu ft per Quikrete).</p><h2>Waste and ordering</h2><ul><li>5% for plain walls with few cuts</li><li>10–15% for walls with many openings, corners or patterns</li></ul><p>Order all bricks for one wall from the same production run so the color matches. For brick or concrete pavers on the ground, use the <a href='/paver-calculator/'>paver calculator</a>; for block walls use the <a href='/concrete-block-calculator/'>concrete block calculator</a>.</p>"""),
}
CATS["Interior"].insert(0,"square-footage-calculator")
CATS["Outdoor & yard"].insert(0,"cubic-yard-calculator")
CATS["Outdoor & yard"].append("board-foot-calculator")
CATS["Concrete & masonry"][1:1]=["concrete-block-calculator","brick-calculator"]
